TY  - JOUR
TI  - Impact of muscle atrophy on bone metabolism and bone strength: implications for muscle-bone crosstalk with aging and disuse.
AU  - Bettis, T.
AU  - Kim, B.-J.
AU  - Hamrick, M. W.
T2  - Osteoporosis international : a journal established as result of cooperation between the European Foundation for Osteoporosis and the National Osteoporosis  Foundation of the USA
AB  - Bone fractures in older adults are often preceded by a loss of muscle mass and strength. Likewise, bone loss with prolonged bed rest, spinal cord injury, or  with exposure to microgravity is also preceded by a rapid loss of muscle mass.  Recent studies using animal models in the setting of hindlimb unloading or  botulinum toxin (Botox) injection also reveal that muscle loss can induce bone  loss. Moreover, muscle-derived factors such as irisin and leptin can inhibit bone  loss with unloading, and knockout of catabolic factors in muscle such as the  ubiquitin ligase Murf1 or the myokine myostatin can reduce osteoclastogenesis.  These findings suggest that therapies targeting muscle in the setting of disuse  atrophy may potentially attenuate bone loss, primarily by reducing bone  resorption. These potential therapies not only include pharmacological approaches  but also interventions such as whole-body vibration coupled with resistance  exercise and functional electric stimulation of muscle.
