AB  - The impact of repeated administration of cinntamtannin A2 (A2, 25 μg/kg) on skeletal muscle disuse atrophy model mice induced by hindlimb suspension for 14  days was examined. In soleus, weight loss and a reduction in the average myofibre  size with shifting to the smaller side of the peak were observed in the  suspension-vehicle group, but A2 reduced these changes. Average myofibre size  significantly increased in ground-A2 compared to ground-vehicle. A marked  increase in the dephosphorylation of forkhead box O (FoxO) 3a by the suspension  was reduced by A2. The phosphorylation of protein kinase B (Akt) and eukaryotic  translation initiation factor 4E-binding protein (4EBP)-1 were significantly  increased by the treatment of A2. In addition, a single dose of A2 increased  dramatically in the 24-h excretion of catecholamines in urine. These results  suggest that A2 administration results in sympathetic nerve activation and  promotes hypertrophy while inhibiting the progress of disuse muscle atrophy.
