TI  - Protective effect of branched chain amino acids on hindlimb suspension-induced muscle atrophy in growing rats.
AU  - Jang, Jiwoong
AU  - Yun, Hea-Yeon
AU  - Park, Jonghoon
AU  - Lim, Kiwon
T2  - Journal of exercise nutrition & biochemistry
AB  - PURPOSE: The effect of BCAA (branched chain amino acid) administration on muscle atrophy during growth phases is not well known. We investigated whether BCAA  administration can prevent the muscle atrophy induced by hindlimb suspension in  growing male rats. METHODS: Male Wistar rats were assigned to 1 of 2 groups (n =  7/group): hindlimb suspension and hindlimb suspension with oral BCAA  administration (600 mg·kg(-1)·day(-1), valine 1: leucine 2: isoleucine 1). After  14 days of hindlimb suspension, the weight and mRNA levels of the soleus muscle  were measured. RESULTS: BCAA administration prevented a decrease in soleus muscle  weight. BCAA administration attenuated atrogin-1 and MuRF1 mRNA expression, which  has been reported to play a pivotal role in muscle atrophy. CONCLUSION: BCAA  could serve as an effective supplement for the prevention or treatment of muscle  atrophy, especially atrophy caused by weightlessness.
